Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) in SQL Server: permissions are assigned to roles, and users are assigned to roles. Fixed database roles: db_owner, db_datareader, db_datawriter, db_ddladmin, etc. Custom roles can be created. Example:
```sql
-- Create custom role
CREATE ROLE LibraryStaff;
-- Grant permissions to role
GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON Book TO LibraryStaff;
GRANT EXECUTE ON sp_IssueBook TO LibraryStaff;
-- Create login and user
CREATE LOGIN staff1 WITH PASSWORD = 'Staff@123';
USE LibraryDB;
CREATE USER staff1 FOR LOGIN staff1;
-- Assign user to role
EXEC sp_addrolemember 'LibraryStaff', 'staff1';
-- Additional: deny permissions if needed
DENY DELETE ON Book TO LibraryStaff;
```

Steps to connect to a database using JDBC with exception handling:
```java
Connection conn = null;
Statement stmt = null;
ResultSet rs = null;
try {
    // 1. Register JDBC driver (optional for JDBC 4+)
    Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
    // 2. Open connection
    String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb";
    conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "user", "password");
    // 3. Create statement
    stmt = conn.createStatement();
    // 4. Execute query
    rs = stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM table");
    // 5. Process results
    while (rs.next()) { /* ... */ }
} catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
} catch (SQLException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
} finally {
    // 6. Close resources
    if (rs != null) try { rs.close(); } catch (SQLException e) { }
    if (stmt != null) try { stmt.close(); } catch (SQLException e) { }
    if (conn != null) try { conn.close(); } catch (SQLException e) { }
}
```
